<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boston Tea Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a> Party was a political protest on 16 December 1773 in which American colonists, some disguised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> Indians, threw 342 chests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> tea into Boston Harbor in defiance of the Tea Act. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> answered with the punitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Coercive Acts</a> of 1774, which hardened colonial resistance and led towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party.jpg" alt="Boston Tea Party" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Cornischong at lb.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boston_Tea_Party_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The protest arose from two problems facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>: the financial difficulties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and a dispute over how far Parliament's authority extended over colonies that elected no members to it. The Townshend Revenue Act of 1767 laid new taxes, which colonists met with protests and boycotts. Meanwhile the company, burdened by taxes, by smuggled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> tea that undersold it and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> famine of 1769 to 1773, amassed a large unsold surplus. Parliament passed the Tea Act on 10 May 1773 to help it.</p><p>The Act in fact lowered the price of legally imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, but Samuel Adams considered the British tea monopoly equal to a tax, and others saw a threat to colonial rights. In every colony except Massachusetts, protesters forced the consignees to resign or the tea to go back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, Governor Thomas Hutchinson held his ground and persuaded the consignees, two of them his sons, not to give way. When the Dartmouth arrived in late November, thousands met on 29 November 1773 at the Old South Meeting House. British law gave the ship twenty days to unload and pay the duties before the cargo could be confiscated. Hutchinson refused to let it leave, and two more ships, the Eleanor and the Beaver, arrived.</p><p>On 16 December, the last day of the deadline, some 5,000 to 7,000 people gathered at Old South. After a report that Hutchinson had again refused, Samuel Adams announced that the meeting could do nothing further to save the country. That evening a large group, some dressed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> warriors and armed with axes, boarded the three vessels at Griffin's Wharf and over three hours emptied all the chests into the harbour, destroying 92,616 pounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> valued at £9,659.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> regarded the act as treason. In 1774 Parliament passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Intolerable Acts</a>, which, among other measures, ended local self-government in Massachusetts and closed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>'s port. The other colonies answered by convening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, which coordinated resistance in the Continental Association of October 1774. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-lexington-and-concord">Battles of Lexington and Concord</a> on 19 April 1775 began the Revolutionary War. The name Boston Tea Party gained currency only in the early 19th century; the event had been called the Destruction of the Tea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boston Tea Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Boston Tea Party</b> of 1773 led to the punitive Coercive Acts, and the colonies coordinated their resistance through the First Continental Congress in 1774.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonists saw it as a ploy to make them accept taxation, leading to the <b>Boston Tea Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-10">10 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They had gathered after the British blockaded Boston Harbor and Parliament passed the Intolerable Acts in answer to the <b>Boston Tea Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-26">26 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance to British taxes grew through the Stamp Act riots, the Boston Massacre of 1770 and the <b>Boston Tea Party</b> of 1773.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Province of Massachusetts Bay</a></li></ul></section>
